{"title":"2004 IMO Shortlist Problems/A3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(<i>Canada</i>)\nDoes there exist a function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/3/733e826166ddc75baaa99928bae772a6c8804b85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s :  \\mathbf{Q} \\rightarrow \\{ -1,1 \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"125\" height=\"18\" /> such that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4e6ba8781d42ee434379ac570532e425e7b55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"9\" height=\"11\" /> are distinct rational numbers satisfying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/6/7865dd958a76c585de5b5254ca720d203e3fea7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle {} xy=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"52\" height=\"15\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/1/711565b44996dbb6bc483e038fefba3d39b6fd9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y \\in \\{ 0,1 \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"18\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/6/1a681739aa7114774a02bda6efb4a756a4de41bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x)s(y) = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />?  Justify your answer.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For a number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, we define the function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/e/cfe1152a6ba649b40d44fbfb7f3659360271bf93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"29\" height=\"18\" /> to be 0, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> is an integer, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/8/ee840daaafd6c1dce605febdd4fc09a4ccc857d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{x-\\lfloor x \\rfloor}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"61\" height=\"42\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> is not an integer.  We use the notation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/3/a9360aae1a2db4408b9fb86ff6e44d9c33c50e8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> to denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/5/975643a82a50f7035377ef49daca21dde6c93bca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t$\" width=\"6\" height=\"11\" /> composed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> times.  We note that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/c/3fc5017f094c888021acacc8ad8662850ac8a1e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x = 0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/2/0e229c2596bf83d3d04abb306387c849fd96f5dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t^n(x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" />, for all nonnegative integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.  We also note that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/b/d5b3c1e7b8eee4d386e329521027373c9db51308.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x,y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> differ by an integer, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/4/5547498bb39da4d79c2eebe594358fead25a7999.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t(x) = t(y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>We claim that for any nonnegative rational <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, there exists a nonnegative number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/2/0e229c2596bf83d3d04abb306387c849fd96f5dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t^n(x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" />.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/b/02bf34e2c764b1c72354fdfd370578174fab513b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p_n, q_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"11\" /> be the relatively prime positive integers such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/b/75b48e225f2b7cf79b6624d13112b65508747da1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t^n(x) = \\frac{p_n}{q_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"85\" height=\"36\" />.  It is enough to show that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/a/a0a58c0a9a53d8386df6ed49c69bed693900e90f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t^n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> is not an integer, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/f/f0f0ae22c75d7851bb151de6f17abf0eb14395c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle q_{n+1} &lt; q_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"14\" />, for then we must eventually have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3b4a67eb100586313915e546a27be296d886f5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle q_n = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/c/73cac6ab82a741de15bdba3d55cfa3fa5aa65aa9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t^{n+1}(x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"19\" />.  But this follows from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/5/b65d3a323966763482715daed62a814d7ef2fdca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t^{n+1}(x) = \\frac{1}{t^n(x) - \\lfloor t^n(x) \\rfloor} = \\frac{q_n}{p_n - q_n \\lfloor p_n/q_n \\rfloor}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"348\" height=\"42\" />, since by definition, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/9/439ef22569c0afd1284f931c583cc58968fb35f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_n - q_n \\lfloor p_n/q_n \\rfloor &lt; q_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"159\" height=\"19\" />.  Thus for nonnegative rational <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, there exists some nonnegative integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/9/aa9779e14187df942ba7f1ece812058cb36831c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t^n(x) =0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"20\" />.  Then for nonnegative rational <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/e/e1ee6271668f057528a84f2236f83e844f1012bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) : \\mathbb{Q} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{Z}/2\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"134\" height=\"18\" /> denote the parity class of the least such integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.  For negative rational <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/e/8be6b32d5564759fef97c21e9548cae08d72b3a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) = f(-x)+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"139\" height=\"18\" />.  We claim that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/9/3c9ec640d65edcb65fe3bf34c604489f1896a30e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x) = (-1)^{f(x)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"119\" height=\"20\" /> satisfies the desired conditions.\n</p><p>First, we prove that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/b/d5b3c1e7b8eee4d386e329521027373c9db51308.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x,y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> are distinct rationals such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/6/7f6afed062a9b5fabef1a8c5ba778a49c1d5f377.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x+y =0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/6/1a681739aa7114774a02bda6efb4a756a4de41bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x)s(y) = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />.  But this follows from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/6/df6a7b9c5202f41f10371089cbbdffb2353b1033.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(-x) \\equiv f(x) + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"139\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Next, we prove that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/b/d5b3c1e7b8eee4d386e329521027373c9db51308.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x,y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> are distinct rationals such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/c/c1c05134bc36641dc3e75b79f8a2905b7500e168.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x+y = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/6/1a681739aa7114774a02bda6efb4a756a4de41bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x)s(y) = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />.  Without loss of generality, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/a/8ba83fcc6bbe5eac36069126d0338a65bbe77189.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x&gt; y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4e6ba8781d42ee434379ac570532e425e7b55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"9\" height=\"11\" /> are integers, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> must be at least 1, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4e6ba8781d42ee434379ac570532e425e7b55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"9\" height=\"11\" /> must be at most 0, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d431abd9448b11026c28cf6414ae5f3662b8e45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x) = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/6/d463a743fa1f5eca042aeff5f8e6fcb66b85f158.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(y)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" />.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> is a non-integer greater than 1, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/4/504f48e0a20da2b131507b4e9807949ed8a21d70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t(x) = t(x-1) = t(-y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"185\" height=\"18\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/7/74777aa221e8d5df6dd2ceb7e37508326f0efe36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x) = s(-y) = -s(y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"172\" height=\"18\" />, as desired.\n</p><p>On the other hand, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> is a positive rational less than one, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4e6ba8781d42ee434379ac570532e425e7b55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"9\" height=\"11\" /> must be positive, and there exist relatively prime positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/7/ed7a134e2a09e43131c9c15bb505ec8919abc8c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p, q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"26\" height=\"11\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/c/0ace7be9efdf8dff7738a75771d81c9a3c026c41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle 2p &lt; q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/4/b34fcada20305dfd6d9a91b3cbfc778050d8f021.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y = \\frac{p}{q} , x= \\frac{q-p}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"132\" height=\"36\" />.  We note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/f/21faa578f39688bd913f9589eff92a1acd3aad3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t(x) = \\frac{q}{q-p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"98\" height=\"36\" />; and since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/9/b69143eb3d688b2b52f24bbcd0dd5e7b50255669.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle \\frac{q}{q-p} = 1 + \\frac{p}{q-p} &lt; 1+ \\frac{p}{2p-p} = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"285\" height=\"36\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/1/b019dedca80a0f397d0a6618b2dd513c4fc6b632.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t^2(x) = \\frac{q-p}{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"105\" height=\"36\" />.  On the other hand, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/8/16832297f7b76e23bf5009bde9375cb3f92c9f8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t(y) = \\frac{q}{p} = \\frac{q-p}{p} + 1 = t^2(x) +1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"260\" height=\"36\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/4/dc4a02c7c6c948b4b583edd2ee62873c7c5c6ce1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t^2(y) = t^3(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"19\" />.  It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/9/da92162dd893ce81ca873f060e1024127fa3a263.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle {} f(x) = f(y) +1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"124\" height=\"18\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/6/1a681739aa7114774a02bda6efb4a756a4de41bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x)s(y) = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />, as desired.\n</p><p>Finally, we prove that for nonzero rational <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/8/d48731ec0b3a3b8b123be82f9a046b9c564c8995.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x)s(1/x) = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"130\" height=\"18\" />, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/6/6e68a577477b6812232e14e5b6d26b33aa6d7b8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x \\neq 1/x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" />.  We may clearly assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/f/2df93276c1baf81dae88eb6c8bee53144ae0e494.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle |x| &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" /> without loss of generality.  We may also assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/f/88fc3a44defab82c1934453732a24d656a8ea877.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x , 1/x &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"80\" height=\"18\" />, since in general, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/e/03e868efc3f372ce1798fc32a61822b9060f73e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x)s(y) = [-s(-x)][-s(-y)] = s(-x)s(-y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"348\" height=\"18\" />.  But we now have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/7/f17c0decfb2dc8e3d218f106e54f5903bf75795a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t(x) = 1/x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/7/9f702e3346d69510ed1ccf5f2cb558351f26aae1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(x) = f(1/x) +1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/8/d48731ec0b3a3b8b123be82f9a046b9c564c8995.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x)s(1/x) = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"130\" height=\"18\" />.  Q.E.D.\n</p><p><br />\n<i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A3&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p><p><b>Comment.</b> The solution's construction may seem arbitrary, but it follows from the observation that if such a function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/c/5bc18328ddad852e8c812a5524cf3a0bd2fe6fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s$\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\" /> exists, then for nonzero <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />,  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/a/a7a15014905f19fef25a9a3fc560126eb051afb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x) = -s(-x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"120\" height=\"20\" />, and indeed we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/c/22c16de432bf639f4c3443391b34ee4de5496034.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(-x) = -s(x+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"148\" height=\"18\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/7/c6733d7999316d175099da6d4aa566a0a87efead.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x) = s(x+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"120\" height=\"18\" />.  Hence for positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/0/5706274ba1188c82a4b77f05b29bd1d26ec4e6a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> must be distinct from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/c/22c7abff044620ac6e6750896c6f343042773f0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{x - k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"45\" height=\"37\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/6/de61dde472a066a5b90a2ff0be7a2f756110011b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle k$\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" /> is any integer less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />: in particular, for non-integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b5a07f2a22fcb2626031da734012c71b549782.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle x$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/d/d9d7142bc572cfa8fd11998645ebe83bea3be068.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = \\lfloor x \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"58\" height=\"19\" />.  The functions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/6/9b67a3073777f3067ba10bdf7bfceb7bf93bf36e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle t,f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" /> encode this condition; the solution constitutes a proof that this set of constraints is consistent with itself.  In fact, from our observations, we can see that our function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/c/5bc18328ddad852e8c812a5524cf3a0bd2fe6fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle s$\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\" /> is unique up to sign.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems\" title=\"2004 IMO Shortlist Problems\">2004 IMO Shortlist Problems</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=188483#188483\">Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017150758\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.030 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.042 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 470/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 353/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 16944/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.341      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n100.00%    5.341      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:5002:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017150758 and revision id 15191. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}